<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Knight in the Panther's Skin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Knight in the Panther's Skin is a Georgian epic poem written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shota-rustaveli">Shota Rustaveli</a> around 1200 and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>. It tells of the knights Avtandil and Tariel and their quest for Tariel's lost beloved, Nestan-Darejan, celebrating friendship, loyalty and love. It is regarded as the national epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-knight-in-the-panthers-skin.jpg" alt="The Knight in the Panther's Skin" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 三猎 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E3%80%8A%E8%99%8E%E7%9A%AE%E6%AD%A6%E5%A3%AB%E3%80%8B%E6%8A%84%E6%9C%AC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem was written during the Golden Age of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>, who had been enthroned by her father, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> of Georgia. Tamar expanded the kingdom to its historical maximum, repulsed invasions and set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> over many Muslim and Christian lands. Georgian trading caravans reached Ayyubid Egypt, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shota-rustaveli">Shota Rustaveli</a>, born in the Meskhetian village of Rustavi, was close to the queen and possibly served as her treasurer. In the prologue he says he wrote the poem to praise Tamar, whom he calls a king. The historian Stephen H. Rapp sees it as an expression of the Iranian epic tradition rather than of Byzantine literature, and Rustaveli calls it a Persian tale translated into Georgian. It runs to over 1,600 quatrains in the shairi verse form.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The story is set in the invented lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Avtandil, commander of the armies of King Rostevan of Arabia, is asked by the king's daughter Tinatin to find a mysterious knight in a panther's skin within three years. After two years and nine months he finds Tariel, son of King Saridan, living in a cave. Tariel had fallen in love with Nestan-Darejan, daughter of King Pharsadan, who was promised to another suitor. At her request he killed the suitor, and she was set adrift on the sea.</p><p>Avtandil swears friendship to Tariel and sets out again to find Nestan-Darejan. In the city of Gulansharo, Patman tells him that Nestan was abducted by Kaji, the demon king. Avtandil, Tariel and Nuradin-Phridon go to the land of Kaji with three hundred men and free her. Avtandil marries Tinatin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, Tariel marries Nestan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the three friends reign in their own realms.</p><p>The poem celebrates friendship, loyalty and courtly love, and condemns forced marriage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Readers see Nestan-Darejan as an allegory of Tamar and the settings as colourful stand-ins for the Georgian kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Until the early 20th century a copy of the poem was part of the dowry of every bride. Its moral framework is Christian, though the poem contains no prayers and no references to Christ, and it refers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> to show that most characters are Muslim. Because Rustaveli presents God as a universal force, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a> in the past destroyed copies, believing him to be a Muslim.</p><p>Interest abroad began in 1802 with a Russian translation of the first stanza. Marie-Félicité Brosset made a partial French translation in 1828, and Marjory Wardrop published the first English translation in 1912. The poem has been translated into dozens of languages, and Lyn Coffin's English version of 2015 keeps the shairi form.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Knight in the Panther's Skin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
